WEBVTT

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.096
Welcome. In this video, you will learn how to use the DRMD AI Generator.

00:00:06.316 --> 00:00:15.622
The generator reads reference material certificates and product information
sheets in PDF format, and turns them into machine-readable DRMD files.

00:00:15.842 --> 00:00:19.864
We will go through every feature, step by step, with a real certificate.

00:00:22.064 --> 00:00:26.439
The generator is a single HTML file. There is nothing to install.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:32.949
Just download the file and open it in Google Chrome
or Microsoft Edge. It runs completely in your browser.

00:00:33.849 --> 00:00:40.645
The screen has two parts. On the left, you will see the certificate.
On the right, you will see the form with the extracted data.

00:00:40.865 --> 00:00:47.849
With the buttons at the top, you can upload one PDF,
upload a whole folder, or import an existing XML file.

00:00:48.069 --> 00:00:51.854
The status bar at the bottom always shows the state of your document.

00:00:52.754 --> 00:01:00.666
The first step is the Settings page. The generator uses Google
Gemini to read the certificates, so it needs a Gemini API key.

00:01:00.886 --> 00:01:08.188
If you do not have a key yet, you can create one in Google AI
Studio. The usage is billed to the account that the key belongs to.

00:01:08.408 --> 00:01:15.797
Paste your key into this field. The key is kept only while this
browser tab is open. It is not saved, and it is sent only to Google.

00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:19.668
When the key is entered, the status bar shows: Gemini key set.

00:01:19.888 --> 00:01:27.490
Below, a short summary explains how the generator works: where your
data is sent, where the results are saved, and which version you are using.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:33.014
Now let us convert a certificate. Click Upload PDF, and choose the file.

00:01:33.234 --> 00:01:44.475
In this example, we use the certificate of the reference material BAM-M386a, pure
copper, from BAM, the German Federal Institute for Materials Research and Testing.

00:01:44.775 --> 00:01:52.408
The generator sends the PDF to Google Gemini. Gemini reads
the whole document and returns the data in a structured form.

00:01:52.628 --> 00:01:57.293
This usually takes less than a minute. For this video, the waiting time is shortened.

00:01:57.093 --> 00:02:02.862
If the answer is incomplete, the generator asks Gemini
again, and tells you if something is still missing.

00:02:03.762 --> 00:02:08.334
When the data is back, the certificate opens on the left, and the form is filled in.

00:02:08.554 --> 00:02:15.577
In the background, the generator now looks up identifiers for the
producer and for each substance. You can already start your review.

00:02:16.477 --> 00:02:22.002
Use the arrows to move between the pages of the
certificate. If a page is turned, you can rotate it.

00:02:22.222 --> 00:02:32.188
The data is organised in tabs, in the same order as a DRMD document:
administrative data, materials, properties, statements, and comments and documents.

00:02:32.408 --> 00:02:35.923
The last tab validates the document and exports the files.

00:02:36.143 --> 00:02:42.382
You can drag the divider to give more space to the certificate
or to the form. Double-click it to go back to the middle.

00:02:43.282 --> 00:02:51.408
Let us start with the administrative data. The title tells whether the
document is a reference material certificate or a product information sheet.

00:02:51.628 --> 00:02:56.243
Every document gets a unique identifier. You can create a new one with this button.

00:02:56.463 --> 00:03:01.809
Fields with a red star are required by the DRMD schema or by the official rules.

00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:09.568
Every field name is also a link. Click a name, and the
DRMD Best Practice guide opens at the matching section.

00:03:09.968 --> 00:03:16.818
Here you can read what the element means. For example,
the unique identifier identifies this document, not the material.

00:03:17.018 --> 00:03:22.788
The guide recommends a UUID, version 4, and a
new one for every new version of the document.

00:03:23.188 --> 00:03:26.039
When you have read the section, switch back to the generator.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:33.235
The period of validity comes from the certificate.
This certificate is valid until the end of 2050.

00:03:33.455 --> 00:03:40.455
Next to most fields, you see a target icon. Click it, and the
generator marks the place in the PDF where the value comes from.

00:03:40.675 --> 00:03:48.713
Here, the certificate says 12/2050. This was converted into the
date format of the XML, and the message shows the conversion.

00:03:49.613 --> 00:04:00.861
Some certificates are valid for a period after dispatch instead. In that case, you enter
the years and months, and the generator writes the period in the ISO 8601 format.

00:04:01.081 --> 00:04:08.827
The dispatch date is the day the material is shipped to the customer, so
it is not on the certificate. The generator always leaves this field empty.

00:04:08.627 --> 00:04:10.968
You can fill it in by hand if you need it.

00:04:13.468 --> 00:04:18.648
The reference material producer is filled in with
the name, the contact details and the address.

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:25.430
Use the target icon to check any field against the
PDF. Here, the email address is marked on page 6.

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:35.025
Here, data enrichment begins. The generator looked up the producer
in the Research Organization Registry, ROR, and added its ROR ID.

00:04:35.245 --> 00:04:40.405
Click the link icon to open the record, and check
that it matches the producer on the certificate.

00:04:40.705 --> 00:04:50.188
The name is Federal Institute for Materials Research and Testing. The other names
show the German name and the acronym, BAM. The location is Berlin, Germany.

00:04:49.988 --> 00:04:51.666
So the ROR ID is correct.

00:04:52.566 --> 00:04:58.047
Back in the generator, the responsible persons are
the people who signed the certificate, with their roles.

00:04:58.267 --> 00:05:03.820
The main signer is ticked. Here, it is Dr. S. Richter, from the Committee for Certification.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:12.292
The Materials tab describes the reference material: its name, the
RM code, the description, the material class, and the quantities.

00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:18.670
Quantities are converted automatically. The
minimum sample size on the certificate is 0.2 grams.

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:24.132
Below the field, you see the same value in the D-SI format, as it is written to the XML.

00:05:24.352 --> 00:05:32.354
When a material is a defined chemical substance, the generator adds
its CAS number and InChIKey here. For this material, none was found.

00:05:33.254 --> 00:05:37.154
The Properties tab holds the measured values, grouped in property sets.

00:05:37.374 --> 00:05:43.319
This set contains the certified values, so Certified
is ticked, and the set is marked with a green line.

00:05:43.539 --> 00:05:49.061
Each property set is linked to one material. This
certificate has only one material, pure copper.

00:05:49.281 --> 00:05:57.210
Every table has a name and a description. The description keeps the
footnotes of the certificate, for example how the uncertainty was calculated.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:06.155
In the table, each row is one measured quantity: the name, the
identifiers, the value, the uncertainty, the unit, and the D-SI unit.

00:06:06.375 --> 00:06:11.099
The last two columns are the coverage factor, k, and the coverage probability, p.

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:22.035
The certificate says that the uncertainty has a coverage factor of k equals 2,
and a level of confidence of about 95 percent. So k is 2, and p is 0.95.

00:06:22.935 --> 00:06:27.703
Now the unit conversion. On the certificate, the unit is milligrams per kilogram (mg/kg).

00:06:27.923 --> 00:06:34.634
The generator converts it automatically into the D-SI unit,
a format that software can read without any doubt.

00:06:34.854 --> 00:06:43.001
If you change a unit, the D-SI unit is updated at once. Here,
grams per kilogram gives a new D-SI unit. Let us change it back.

00:06:43.901 --> 00:06:50.260
For every element, the generator also looked up two
identifiers: the CAS Registry Number and the InChIKey.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:55.132
Click a column name like Identifiers to read about it in the Best Practice guide.

00:06:54.932 --> 00:07:00.464
Property identifiers make each measured quantity
machine-readable, for example with a CAS number.

00:07:01.464 --> 00:07:10.651
Each identifier is a link. The CAS number opens the substance in CAS
Common Chemistry, and the InChIKey opens the compound in PubChem.

00:07:10.751 --> 00:07:19.152
On the page that opens, check that the substance name matches the row,
here silver, and that the identifier is exactly the same as in the generator.

00:07:19.372 --> 00:07:23.601
In PubChem, you find the InChIKey in the section Computed Descriptors.

00:07:24.501 --> 00:07:31.730
Further down is a second property set with values for information.
These values are not certified, so Certified is not ticked.

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:35.718
You can add rows, tables and property sets by hand at any time.

00:07:36.618 --> 00:07:46.160
Now an important feature. The generator compares every extracted value with the
text of the PDF. If a value does not appear word for word, it is outlined in amber.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.308
The tab and the status bar show how many values are left to verify.

00:07:50.528 --> 00:07:58.881
Click the value to see where it comes from. Here, the uncertainty of sulfur,
1.8, was not found word for word, so compare it with the certificate.

00:07:59.081 --> 00:08:05.770
The certificate shows 1.8 for sulfur. The value is
correct, so click the small box to mark it as verified.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:13.606
The amber outline disappears, and the counters go down.
Antimony has the same value, 1.8, so it is verified together with it.

00:08:13.406 --> 00:08:17.043
If you edit a value yourself, it also counts as verified.

00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:24.044
If a PDF is a scan without a text layer, values cannot be
compared automatically, and the status bar tells you so.

00:08:24.944 --> 00:08:36.202
The Statements tab holds the standard statements that ISO 17034 expects on a
certificate, such as the intended use, storage, handling, and metrological traceability.

00:08:36.422 --> 00:08:41.048
As before, the target icon shows where each statement comes from in the PDF.

00:08:41.268 --> 00:08:45.272
Anything else that the certificate states can be added under Other statements.

00:08:46.172 --> 00:08:56.013
On the Comment and documents tab, a general comment is added automatically.
It tells the reader to refer to the PDF certificate for more information. You can change it.

00:08:56.233 --> 00:09:02.932
The certificate itself is attached automatically and embedded
in the XML file, so you do not need to add it yourself.

00:09:03.152 --> 00:09:11.435
If you have more documents, for example a certification report,
you can attach them here, as PDF files of up to 200 megabytes each.

00:09:12.335 --> 00:09:22.321
Finally, open Validate and export. The generator checks the document
against the DRMD schema, version 1.0.0, and the official business rules.

00:09:22.541 --> 00:09:24.873
This document is ready to export.

00:09:25.093 --> 00:09:31.817
The validation report lists every finding. An error must
be fixed before export. A check is a conditional rule.

00:09:31.617 --> 00:09:39.163
It applies only if the case is relevant for your material, for
example a commutability statement. A note is a recommendation.

00:09:40.063 --> 00:09:43.367
The Best Practice guide explains these levels in detail.

00:09:43.567 --> 00:09:49.152
For a conditional rule, ask yourself: does this apply
to my material? If it does, add the information.

00:09:48.952 --> 00:09:53.639
If it does not, it is better to say so in the document than to leave the element out.

00:09:54.539 --> 00:10:02.404
Let us see what an error looks like. If the document version is deleted,
the tab and the status bar turn red, and the report says what is missing.

00:10:02.704 --> 00:10:05.987
When the version is entered again, the document is valid again.

00:10:06.887 --> 00:10:14.307
Here you see the XML itself, and you can copy it. Below,
the HTML report shows the same data in a readable form.

00:10:14.527 --> 00:10:25.956
Click DRMD XML to download the file. It is named after the RM code:
BAM-M386a.xml. With HTML report, you download the report as well.

00:10:26.176 --> 00:10:32.585
To publish the certificate in the COMAR database,
download both files, sign in to COMAR, and upload them there.

00:10:32.805 --> 00:10:38.404
Please remember: data extracted with AI must
always be reviewed before you use or publish it.

00:10:39.604 --> 00:10:45.065
To convert many certificates at once, click Upload
folder. A short note explains what happens.

00:10:45.285 --> 00:10:50.774
You choose the folder that holds the PDF files. Files in subfolders are included too.

00:10:50.994 --> 00:11:02.063
For every PDF, the DRMD XML and the HTML report are saved into the same
folder, right next to it, with the same name. Your PDF files stay as they are.

00:11:02.283 --> 00:11:08.956
Next, your browser asks for permission to edit files in this
folder. Choose Allow, so that the results can be saved.

00:11:09.656 --> 00:11:16.931
The certificates are processed one after another. Each result is
saved as soon as it is ready, so nothing piles up in the browser.

00:11:17.151 --> 00:11:25.045
If a run stops, simply upload the same folder again. Certificates that
already have an XML file are skipped, and shown as saved earlier.

00:11:25.945 --> 00:11:28.844
To see the whole table, drag the divider to the left.

00:11:28.944 --> 00:11:35.080
The status column shows the result for every file.
This certificate is valid, with conditional rules to check.

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:44.339
For this product information sheet, the AI's answer was incomplete: the producer
is missing. Retry processes such files again, or you can complete them by hand.

00:11:44.559 --> 00:11:50.435
In the folder, every PDF now has its XML file and HTML report next to it.

00:11:50.655 --> 00:11:59.378
Click Review to open a result next to its PDF. With Previous and Next, you
move through the folder. Your changes are saved to the folder automatically.

00:11:59.598 --> 00:12:01.876
Back to list returns to the results.

00:12:02.876 --> 00:12:08.385
To continue working on a file later, click Import XML, and choose a DRMD file.

00:12:08.605 --> 00:12:15.435
The form is filled in again, and the embedded certificate
opens on the left. You can review, validate and export it again.

00:12:16.335 --> 00:12:26.057
That is the DRMD AI Generator. Upload a PDF, review the data next to the
certificate, check the enriched units and identifiers, validate, and export.

00:12:26.277 --> 00:12:32.006
For details about every element, see the DRMD
Best Practice guide. Thank you for watching.
